@meta
id: 11-07
title: Drill — comment out eight lines
chapter: 11
type: drill
skills: dot-design
par: 29
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: select the block, Ctrl+slash
open: debug.ts

@brief
Eight lines of debug logging to comment out before this goes near a
review. Prefix each of the eight with `// `, leaving the indentation
alone.

One change at the top, then `j.` to the bottom.

@keys
| `I` | insert before the first non-blank character of the line |
| `Esc` | ends the change |
| `.` | again |

@hints
`I` is not the same as `0i`. It lands after the indentation, which is
where a comment marker belongs.
--
Drop to the first `console` line, type the marker once, and never type it
again.

@solution
    4j  I// <Esc>   then  j.  seven times

`I` and `A` are a pair worth having in the fingers: the start of the
content and the end of the line, from any column, in one key. Because
neither depends on where the cursor was, both replay perfectly under `.`.

Commenting a block out is the everyday case, and it is one of the few
places where vim's answer is genuinely shorter than the editor shortcut
you came from — `j.` per line, no selection to make first and none to
clear afterwards.

@start debug.ts
export class ReconcileTask {
  public async run() {
    const batch = await this.orderRepository.findPending();

    console.log('batch size', batch.length);
    console.log('first order', batch[0]?.id);
    console.log('last order', batch[batch.length - 1]?.id);
    console.table(batch.map((order) => order.status));
    console.log('customers', batch.map((order) => order.customerId));
    console.time('reconcile');
    console.log('ledger account', LEDGER_ACCOUNT);
    console.timeEnd('reconcile');

    return this.reconcile(batch);
  }
}

